  <turn role="assistant" model="claude-opus-5-5" tokens="28308" effort="medium" commit="83311dd53096f6b06e9552881bffe3920bdd4458">
    <text>No, a comma-separated list won't work. Each A record holds exactly one IP address, so you make four separate records with the same host:

| Type | Host | Value | TTL |
|---|---|---|---|
| A Record | @ | 185.199.108.153 | Automatic |
| A Record | @ | 185.199.109.153 | Automatic |
| A Record | @ | 185.199.110.153 | Automatic |
| A Record | @ | 185.199.111.153 | Automatic |

In Namecheap, click **Add New Record** four times. Before adding them, **remove the `CNAME @` record**. Namecheap won't let a CNAME and an A record exist on the same host, so it may refuse the A records or act strangely while the CNAME is still there.

Keep the `CNAME www → tingofurro.github.io.` record.

Expect a short window of a few minutes, up to the TTL, where the domain may not resolve while the change spreads. After that, the Settings → Pages check should still pass, and Enforce HTTPS will keep working.</text>
  </turn>
  <turn role="user" timestamp="2026-11-10T15:33:33Z" permissionMode="auto">
    <text>why do I need these 4 and not just 1</text>
  </turn>
  <turn role="assistant" model="claude-opus-5-5" tokens="28585" effort="medium" thinkingMs="144">
    <text>One would work, but the four are for redundancy. Those four IPs are separate front-end entry points into GitHub's Pages network.

- **Failover:** when a domain has several A records, browsers and DNS resolvers get the whole list. If one IP is unreachable because of maintenance, an outage or a routing problem, they try the next one. With only one IP, your site goes down whenever that address has a problem.
- **Load spreading:** resolvers often rotate the order of the list, so traffic gets spread across the four.
- **It's what GitHub supports:** GitHub documents all four as the official set. If they ever retire or move one, you'd still be covered by the other three.

So a single record would work today, but your site would be less reliable than it needs to be. Adding all four costs nothing.</text>
  </turn>
